"Optimization of the second Robin eigenvalue on a planar exterior domain under fixed area constraint (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.34320)\nciteturn7academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Title: Optimization of the second Robin eigenvalue on a planar exterior domain under fixed area constraint ... We prove that under a fixed area constraint and convexity assumption on the bounded complement of the exterior domain the second Robin eigenvalue is maximized by the exterior of the disk. ... This result resolves the first item in a 2024 conjecture by the authors under an additional convexity assumption.\nTitle: Optimization of the second Robin eigenvalue on a planar exterior domain under fixed area constraint\nAuthors: David Krejcirik, Vladimir Lotoreichik\nDate: Sun Oct 25 14:31:33 2026\n\nWe consider the Robin Laplacian on a planar exterior domain with a negative boundary parameter. For a negative boundary parameter of a sufficiently large absolute value this operator has at least two negative eigenvalues below the bottom of the essential spectrum. We prove that under a fixed area constraint and convexity assumption on the bounded complement of the exterior domain the second Robin eigenvalue is maximized by the exterior of the disk. The inequality is strict for every exterior of a non-circular domain whenever the comparison disk has a negative second eigenvalue. The proof modifies the parallel-coordinate method by allowing the angular phase of a trial function to vary with the distance from the boundary. This result resolves the first item in a 2024 conjecture by the authors under an additional convexity assumption. Relaxing the convexity and the perimeter constraint remain open problems.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSecond Robin eigenvalue optimization conjecture | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/414137/second-robin-eigenvalue-optimization-conjecture)\nciteturn7search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; Krejčiřík and Lotoreichik formulated the conjecture in 2024: among simply connected planar domains with fixed area or fixed perimeter, the exterior of a disk should maximize the second Robin eigenvalue. ... Current status (as of October 2026): The fixed-area conjecture is claimed proved for convex domains, while the general fixed-area problem and the fixed-perimeter problem remain open.\n\n# Second Robin eigenvalue optimization conjecture\n\nLet $\\alpha<0$α<0, and let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb{R}^{2}$Ω⊂R 2 be a bounded simply connected domain such that the exterior domain $E=\\mathbb{R}^{2}\\setminus\\overline{\\Omega}$E=R 2∖Ω has a second negative Robin eigenvalue $\\lambda_{2}^{\\alpha}(E)$λ 2 α​(E) for the Robin Laplacian with boundary condition $\\partial_{\\nu}u+\\alpha u=0$∂ν​u+α u=0. Among such domains with fixed area $|\\Omega|$∣Ω∣, conjecture that $\\lambda_{2}^{\\alpha}(E)$λ 2 α​(E) is maximized by the exterior of the disk $B$B satisfying $|B|=|\\Omega|$∣B∣=∣Ω∣; that is, $\\lambda_{2}^{\\alpha}(\\mathbb{R}^{2}\\setminus\\overline{\\Omega})\\leq\\lambda_{2}^{\\alpha}(\\mathbb{R}^{2}\\setminus\\overline{B})$λ 2 α​(R 2∖Ω)≤λ 2 α​(R 2∖B). The analogous conjecture asserts the same inequality when the perimeter of $\\Omega$Ω is fixed, with $B$B chosen to have the same perimeter. The area inequality is proved for convex $\\Omega$Ω, while the general area case and the perimeter case remain open.\n\nReferences\n\n  * Optimization of the second Robin eigenvalue on a planar exterior domain under fixed area constraint — arXiv — David Krejcirik, Vladimir Lotoreichik\n\nSource: arXiv\n\npartial-differential-equationsshape-optimizationspectral-geometry\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Refreshed 2 days ago\n\nOpen · progress reported\n\nA new unrefereed preprint proves the conjecture only for convex shapes; the general area and perimeter cases remain open.\n\nKrejčiřík and Lotoreichik formulated the conjecture in 2024: among simply connected planar domains with fixed area or fixed perimeter, the exterior of a disk should maximize the second Robin eigenvalue. Neither conjectured inequality was previously proved in general.\n\nKnown results\n\n  * Krejčiřík and Lotoreichik (2024): the disk optimizer was proved under a fixed elastic-energy constraint for convex domains.\n  * Krejčiřík and Lotoreichik (2024): a related monotonicity theorem holds for strictly star-shaped, centrally symmetric domains containing a disk.\n  * The fixed-area and fixed-perimeter conjectures remained open for general simply connected domains, including nonconvex ones.\n\nOctober 2026 convex-domain advance\n\nA preprint by David Krejčiřík and Vladimir Lotoreichik claims the fixed-area statement for convex exterior domains, using a parallel-coordinate argument with a distance-dependent angular phase. This is claimed progress toward the conjecture, not a solution of the nonconvex or fixed-perimeter cases.\n\nCurrent status (as of October 2026): The fixed-area conjecture is claimed proved for convex domains, while the general fixed-area problem and the fixed-perimeter problem remain open.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOptimisation and monotonicity of the second Robin eigenvalue on a planar exterior domain (https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.14286)\nciteturn7academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.3 years ago; Title: Optimisation and monotonicity of the second Robin eigenvalue on a planar exterior domain ... We state a general conjecture that the second eigenvalue is maximised by the exterior of a disk under isochoric or isoperimetric constraints.\nTitle: Optimisation and monotonicity of the second Robin eigenvalue on a planar exterior domain\nAuthors: David Krejcirik, Vladimir Lotoreichik\nDate: Wed Jul 26 16:42:09 2023\n\nWe consider the Laplace operator in the exterior of a compact set in the plane, subject to Robin boundary conditions. If the boundary coupling is sufficiently negative, there are at least two discrete eigenvalues below the essential spectrum. We state a general conjecture that the second eigenvalue is maximised by the exterior of a disk under isochoric or isoperimetric constraints. We prove an isoelastic version of the conjecture for the exterior of convex domains. Finally, we establish a monotonicity result for the second eigenvalue under the condition that the compact set is strictly star-shaped and centrally symmetric.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe extremal-rank mutation conjecture for nondegenerate Jacobi-finite QPs | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/357788/the-extremal-rank-mutation-conjecture-for-nondegenerate-jaco)\nciteturn7search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; The extremal-rank mutation conjecture for nondegenerate Jacobi-finite QPs ... Jiarui Fei, “On the General Ranks of QP Representations”, arXiv:2303.10591 (2024).\n\nThe extremal-rank mutation conjecture for nondegenerate Jacobi-finite QPs\n\nLet $(Q,\\mathcal{S})$(Q,S) be a nondegenerate Jacobi-finite QP. For a pair $(\\delta,\\epsilon)$(δ,ϵ) of $\\delta$δ-vectors of $(Q,\\mathcal{S})$(Q,S), say that the pair has completely extremal rank when\n\n$$\\operatorname{rank}(\\delta,\\epsilon)=0,\\quad \\operatorname{rank}(\\delta,\\epsilon)=\\underline{\\dim}(\\delta),\\quad\\text{or}\\quad \\operatorname{rank}(\\delta,\\epsilon)=\\underline{\\dim}(\\epsilon).$$rank(δ,ϵ)=0,rank(δ,ϵ)=dim​(δ),or rank(δ,ϵ)=dim​(ϵ).\n\nHere $\\underline{\\dim}(\\delta)$dim​(δ) denotes the dimension vector of a general representation in $\\operatorname{PC}(\\delta)$PC(δ). Extremal-rank mutation conjecture. For any pair $(\\delta,\\epsilon)$(δ,ϵ) of $\\delta$δ-vectors, there is a sequence of mutations $\\mu_{\\boldsymbol{u}}$μ u​ such that $(\\mu_{\\boldsymbol{u}}(\\delta),\\mu_{\\boldsymbol{u}}(\\epsilon))$(μ u​(δ),μ u​(ϵ)) has completely extremal rank. This conjecture would make the mutation-based computation of general ranks effective; the source also records that the authors were not completely confident about it. The supplied parser status is unknown, so it remains open in this database.\n\n## Primary source\n\nJiarui Fei, “On the General Ranks of QP Representations”, arXiv:2303.10591 (2024).\n\ncluster-algebrasquiver-representationsquivers-with-potentials\n\n0\n\n1 week ago\n\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOptimization of the second Robin eigenvalue on a planar exterior domain under fixed area constraint | Cool Papers - Immersive Paper Discovery (https://papers.cool/arxiv/2610.34320)\nciteturn7search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: 5 days ago; ## #1 Optimization of the second Robin eigenvalue on a planar exterior domain under fixed area constraint [PDF^{}] [Copy] [Kimi^{}] [REL] ... This result resolves the first item in a 2024 conjecture by the authors under an additional convexity assumption.\n\n## #1 Optimization of the second Robin eigenvalue on a planar exterior domain under fixed area constraint [PDF^{}] [Copy] [Kimi^{}] [REL]\n\nAuthors: David Krejcirik, Vladimir Lotoreichik\n\nWe consider the Robin Laplacian on a planar exterior domain with a negative boundary parameter. For a negative boundary parameter of a sufficiently large absolute value this operator has at least two negative eigenvalues below the bottom of the essential spectrum. We prove that under a fixed area constraint and convexity assumption on the bounded complement of the exterior domain the second Robin eigenvalue is maximized by the exterior of the disk. The inequality is strict for every exterior of a non-circular domain whenever the comparison disk has a negative second eigenvalue. The proof modifies the parallel-coordinate method by allowing the angular phase of a trial function to vary with the distance from the boundary. This result resolves the first item in a 2